Thin specimens for TEM were cut from the heat-treated blanks, and ground to about 60 μm using conventional techniques. The foils for TEM studies were prepared by standard electropolishing. Electropolishing was performed in a Tenupol Model twin-jet electropolishing unit, using a solution of 50 ml perchloric acid, 600 ml methanol and 350 ml butyl alcohol at a temperature of −40 °C. TEM studies were conducted in a JEOL-F200 microscope operating at an accelerating voltage of 200 kV, equipped with a JEOL Dual-EDS system. The microstructural analyses were performance in STEM mode in order to reduce diffraction effects on the dislocation distribution. The dislocation density was measured by the intersection counting technique. Lines of a known total length were drawn on the image, and the number of intersections with the dislocations was counted. The dislocation density, ρ, was then calculated using the formula: 7 ρ = 2 n l t \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
